===Etymology===
From {{etyl|enm|en}}, from {{etyl|ang|en}} {{term|hogg|lang=ang}}, {{term|hocg||hog|lang=ang}}, possibly from {{etyl|non|en}} {{term|hǫggva||to strike, chop, cut|lang=non|sc=Latinx}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{m|gem-pro|*hawwaną||to hew, forge}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|en}} {{m|ine-pro|*kowə-||to beat, hew, forge}}. Cognate with Old High German {{term|houwan|lang=goh}}, Old Saxon {{term|hauwan|lang=osx}}, Old English {{m|ang|hēawan}} (English {{term|hew|lang=en}}). "Hog" originally meant a [[castrate]]d male pig. (Compare "[[hoggett]]" for a castrated male sheep.) More at {{l|en|hew}}.

